    ":question/math-academy-id": "q-140312",
    ":question/problem": "Rewrite the expression ${x}^{2}-7x+8$ as the sum of the perfect square and a constant.",
    ":question/worked-solution": "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+8\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}$, writing the constant term $8$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}}-{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}+8\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}+8\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{17}{4}.\n$$"
  },
