    ":question/math-academy-id": "q-223640",
    ":question/problem": "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${w}^{2}-3w+3.$\n\n${w}^{2}-3w+3=(w\\,\\,-$\n{{field-1}} $)^{2}+$\n{{field-2}}",
    ":question/worked-solution": "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{w}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}w+3\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}$, writing the constant term $3$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{w}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}w+{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}}-\\,{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(w-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(w-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$"
  },
