    ":question/id": "aae80fdb-9519-54f8-9106-0189ec7972b1",
    ":question/problem": "Rewrite $7-5z+z^2$ in the form $(z+a)^2+b$. Find the signed numbers $a$ and $b$.\n\n$a=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$b=$ {{field-2}}",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Put the quadratic term first: $7-5z+z^2=z^2-5z+7$. The half of the linear coefficient is $-\\frac{5}{2}$, so the square must be $\\left(z-\\frac{5}{2}\\right)^2$.\n\nAdd and subtract the square of that half:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz^2-5z+7&=\\underbrace{z^2-2\\cdot\\frac{5}{2}z+\\frac{25}{4}}_{\\text{perfect square}}-\\frac{25}{4}+7\\\\\n&=\\left(z-\\frac{5}{2}\\right)^2+\\frac{3}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing with $(z+a)^2+b$ gives $a=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $b=\\frac{3}{4}$.\n\nCheck by expanding:\n\n$$\n\\left(z-\\frac{5}{2}\\right)^2+\\frac{3}{4}=z^2-5z+\\frac{25}{4}+\\frac{3}{4}=z^2-5z+7.\n$$"
